output.Part 4:
{
    "Part 4": "Transcription Factor Activity",
    "steps": [
        {
            "implementation details": "I. Estimation of transcription factor activity through target gene enrichment analysis:\n\u2460 The transcription factor activity is indirectly estimated by assessing the activity of target genes using the Transcription Factor Target Enrichment Analysis (TFTEA) method. This method provides a framework for evaluating the influence of transcription factor activity on gene expression changes observed in sarcoma samples.\n\u2461 A total of 130 transcription factor targets along the circuits associated with survival were identified, with 65 of these being dysregulated. The selection of these targets was based on their relevance to the signaling circuits previously identified, with parameters and thresholds established in the TFTEA methodology.",
            "original text": "Given the low expression levels exhibited by transcription factors (TF), the observed gene expression values in transcriptomic experiments may not represent their appropriate activity in the cell. A workaround to indirectly estimate the activity of a TF is to assess the activity of its target genes using any enrichment method. Here, the TFTEA [27] method was used. A total of 130 TF targets along the circuits associated with survival were found, 65 of which were dysregulated."
        },
        {
            "implementation details": "II. Analysis of transcription factor targets:\n\u2460 The results indicate that the most shared transcription factors by sarcoma subtypes have target genes that are overrepresented in the overexpressed part of the rank. This highlights the potential importance of these transcription factors in the progression of sarcoma.\n\u2461 SPI1 (a.k.a. PU.1) is noted as one of the transcription factors whose targets are present in most of the circuits, with significant dysregulation observed in several key genes such as PIK3CG and AKT2, which are central components of the PI3K/Akt signaling network present in various pathways. The analysis underscores how these factors contribute to the overall signaling landscape in sarcoma.",
            "original text": "The results indicate that the most shared transcription factors by sarcoma subtypes have target genes overrepresented in the overexpressed part of the rank (Table 2 and Table S2). SPI1 (a.k.a. PU.1) is the TF whose targets are present in most of the circuits. In particular, it has 41 targets within the signaling circuits whose expression is dysregulated, including PIK3CG and AKT2, central components of the PI3K/Akt signaling network present in various pathways, as well as components of the MAPK signaling network such as IKBKB, MAPK10, or RAC2."
        }
    ]
}

output.Part 1:
{
    "Part 1": "Samples",
    "steps": [
        {
            "implementation details": "I. Selection of tumor and normal tissue samples from TCGA and GTEx projects:\n\u2460 Tumor Samples: Included 206 sarcoma samples from the TCGA SARC project, encompassing seven subtypes: 50 dedifferentiated liposarcomas (DDLPS), 80 leiomyosarcomas (including 53 soft tissue STLMS and 27 uterine ULMS), 5 malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors (MPNST), 17 myxofibrosarcomas (MFS), 10 synovial sarcomas (SS), and 44 undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcomas (UPS).\n\u2461 Normal Samples: Included non-diseased sarcomagenic tissues from the GTEx project, specifically selected for their relevance to sarcomagenic tissues based on criteria such as tissue type and origin.",
            "original text": "Selected data from GTEx tissues and the SARC project were downloaded. Samples from GTEx on putative sarcomagenic tissues of mesenchymal origin were: 1293 adipose, 520 fibroblasts, 881 skeletal muscle, 553 esophagus muscularis, 384 stomach, 159 uterus, and 946 vessels. From TCGA, the 206 sarcoma samples include 7 subtypes: 50 dedifferentiated liposarcomas (DDLPS), 80 leiomyosarcomas (divided into 53 soft tissue STLMS and 27 uterine ULMS), 5 malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors (MPNST), 17 myxofibrosarcomas (MFS), 10 synovial sarcomas (SS), and 44 undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma (UPS)."
        },
        {
            "implementation details": "II. Data Preprocessing:\n\u2460 RNA-seq Data: Raw gene counts for tumor samples were obtained from the recount3 resource. The RNA-Seq dataset counts were uniformly processed and aligned to the UCSC hg38 assembly using STAR and Megadepth. Quality control measures included filtering out low-quality and non-primary tumor samples.\n\u2461 Microarray Data: Normalization was performed using the MAS5 algorithm from the 'affy' R package, and the data was mapped to NCBI Entrez gene identifiers. Only high-quality protein-coding genes were retained for analysis.\n\u2462 scRNA-seq Data: Demultiplexing, read alignment, and feature-barcode matrix generation were conducted using CellRanger (v6.0.0, 10x Genomics). Cells with mitochondrial gene expression exceeding 15%, or with fewer than 200 or more than 9000 expressed genes, were excluded from the analysis to maintain sample integrity.",
            "original text": "Raw gene counts for tumor and normal tissue samples were obtained from the recount3 R package. Recount3 provides RNA-Seq dataset counts where raw reads from multiple sources, including cancer and non-diseased tissues generated by the TCGA and the Genotype-Tissue Expression Project (GTEx), respectively, were uniformly processed with a pipeline using STAR and Megadepth to align and quantify the reads to the UCSC hg38 assembly. Low-quality and non-primary tumor samples from the sarcoma project were filtered out."
        },
        {
            "implementation details": "III. Sample Description:\n\u2460 Tumor Samples: The study utilized 206 tumor samples from the TCGA SARC project, categorized into seven distinct subtypes. Each subtype was selected based on expert pathology reviews to ensure accurate classification.\n\u2461 Normal Tissues: Non-diseased samples were sourced from the GTEx project, specifically selected for their relevance to sarcomagenic tissues, such as adipose, fibroblasts, and muscle tissues, ensuring a strong comparison for downstream analyses.",
            "original text": "The downloaded raw counts for all samples were normalized using the Trimmed Mean of M-values (TMM) method, as implemented in the edgeR package version 3.32.1. Normalized counts in the log scale were obtained with the voom function provided in the limma package version 3.46.0."
        }
    ]
}

output.Part 8:
{
    "Part 8": "Mechanistic Modeling of Signal Transduction",
    "steps": [
        {
            "implementation details": "I. Mechanistic Model Setup:\n\u2460 The mechanistic model is designed to replicate the dynamics of signaling circuits defined within physiological pathways, emphasizing the interconnected nature of these pathways in tumorigenesis.\n\u2461 Each signaling circuit is represented as a directed graph, establishing connections from receptor proteins to effector proteins through a series of activations and inhibitions mediated by intermediate proteins. This structure underscores the causal relationships essential for understanding cancer biology.\n\u2462 The model considers gene expression levels as proxies for the activity of their corresponding proteins, establishing a causal link between gene activity and cellular outcomes. This approach is critical for identifying potential therapeutic targets.",
            "original text": "The mechanistic model replicates the dynamics of the signaling circuits defined within pathways. These circuits are depicted as directed graphs, establishing connections from receptor to effector proteins through a series of activations and inhibitions mediated by intermediate proteins. Ultimately, effector proteins at the end of these circuits initiate distinct cellular functions. Malfunctions of these circuits can trigger cancer hallmarks in neoplastic cells."
        },
        {
            "implementation details": "II. Signal Intensity Calculation:\n\u2460 The signal intensity for each node in the circuit is calculated using a recursive rule based on the normalized gene expression values and the activation/inhibition signals from connected nodes. This calculation is crucial for modeling the dynamics of signal transduction activity along these circuits, linking gene expression variations to functional outcomes.\n\u2461 To handle missing data, imputation methods should be employed based on the average expression levels from the dataset, ensuring robustness in the analysis.",
            "original text": "For a circuit to become active and effectively transmit the signal triggering a specific function, it necessitates the concurrent presence of the entire chain of proteins connecting the receptor to the effector while ensuring the absence of inhibitory proteins that might impede signal propagation along the circuit."
        },
        {
            "implementation details": "III. Pathway Activity Calculation:\n\u2460 Prior to calculating pathway activity, normalized gene expression values are rescaled to the range of 0\u20131 using HiPathia\u2019s normalization methods. This step is vital for ensuring comparability across different samples.\n\u2461 HiPathia is employed to transform gene expression data into the activity of signaling pathways, defining individual circuits that drive specific cellular functions. Outputs from HiPathia should be interpreted with regard to their biological significance in tumor biology.\n\u2462 The activity values are visualized using dimensionality reduction techniques like t-SNE and heatmaps, facilitating the exploration of high-dimensional data and enabling intuitive understanding of the signaling landscape.",
            "original text": "Prior to pathway activity computation, normalized values were rescaled to the range 0\u20131 using the function normalize_data from the package HiPathia. HiPathia (HIgh throughput PATHway Interpretation and Analysis) is designed to transform gene count data into the activity of signaling pathways divided into single-effector circuits that drive specific cell functions."
        },
        {
            "implementation details": "IV. Survival Analysis Integration:\n\u2460 Cox proportional hazards models are used to explore the association between the activation of signaling circuits and patient survival. This statistical approach allows for the identification of significant predictors of survival based on the modeled circuit activities.\n\u2461 Kaplan\u2013Meier survival curves are generated based on circuit activity levels, allowing for the visualization of survival outcomes associated with specific signaling activities. This analysis provides insights into the potential prognostic value of the identified circuits.",
            "original text": "Survival analysis was performed on sarcoma samples using the survival R package. From this package, Cox proportional hazards regression models were computed to estimate differences in patient survival in association with circuit activity or gene expression values."
        },
        {
            "implementation details": "V. Differential Expression and Activity Analysis:\n\u2460 Differential comparisons between sarcoma subtypes and normal tissues are conducted using the limma package, which applies linear model fitting and empirical Bayes methods to identify differentially expressed genes and activated circuits. This step is crucial for understanding how signaling pathways are altered in cancer.\n\u2461 The results from this analysis provide insights into the signaling pathways that are specifically altered in each sarcoma subtype compared to normal tissues, highlighting potential therapeutic targets.",
            "original text": "For differential comparisons between sarcoma subtypes and normal tissues of both normalized expression and activity values, limma was used. Limma pipeline consists of linear model fitting followed by standard error moderation with an empirical Bayes method."
        }
    ]
}

